return [
 ['why-period-wise-attendance-beats-daily-roll-call', 'Why period-wise attendance beats a single daily roll call', 'A child can be present at 8 AM and gone by 10. Only period-wise records reveal it.', 'Attendance',
  '<p>Most schools mark attendance once, in the morning. It is quick, but it hides the most important question: <b>was the child actually in class?</b></p>
<h2>What a daily roll call misses</h2>
<ul><li>Students who leave after the first or second period</li><li>Children who skip one particular subject again and again</li><li>Late arrivals that never get recorded</li><li>Patterns that show a child is losing interest or facing a problem</li></ul>
<h2>What period-wise attendance adds</h2>
<p>When each teacher marks their own period, the school gets a map of the day. A pattern such as "always absent in Period 5 on Fridays" is easy to spot and talk about with the family.</p>
<blockquote>Attendance data is not for punishment. It is an early warning that a child may need help.</blockquote>
<h2>Is it a burden on teachers?</h2>
<p>Not when the screen is designed well. In the portal, everyone starts as <b>Present</b> and the teacher only taps the few who are absent or late. It takes under a minute per period.</p>
<h3>Tips for a smooth start</h3>
<ol><li>Start with one or two classes as a pilot.</li><li>Mark attendance in the first five minutes of the period.</li><li>Review the weekly report with class teachers.</li><li>Talk to parents early, kindly and with facts.</li></ol>'],
 ['explaining-the-75-percent-attendance-rule', 'How to explain the 75% attendance rule to parents', 'A clear, friendly way to talk about minimum attendance before it becomes a problem.', 'Parents',
  '<p>Many schools expect students to maintain a minimum attendance, commonly around <b>75%</b>, to be eligible for examinations. Parents often hear about it only when a child is already short.</p>
<h2>Explain it early</h2>
<p>Mention the rule at the first parent-teacher meeting and again in a notice. Use simple numbers: out of 100 periods, a child must attend at least 75.</p>
<h2>Show the percentage, not just the rule</h2>
<p>When parents can see their child\'s current percentage, subject by subject, the rule stops being abstract. A parent dashboard makes this possible at any time, not just at report-card day.</p>
<h2>Use alerts to prevent, not to punish</h2>
<ul><li>Send a same-day message when a child is absent</li><li>Send a gentle reminder when the percentage drops near the limit</li><li>Invite the family to talk if there is a genuine difficulty such as illness</li></ul>
<h2>Sample wording</h2>
<blockquote>Dear Parent, attendance helps your child learn steadily. Your child is currently at 78%. Please help us keep it above 75%. If there is any difficulty, we are here to help.</blockquote>
<p>Tone matters. A respectful message gets cooperation; a threatening one gets silence.</p>'],
 ['move-your-school-register-online-in-30-minutes', 'A 30-minute checklist to move your school register online', 'You can set up the basics in one sitting. Here is the order that works.', 'Guides',
  '<p>Going digital sounds big, but the first step is small. Use this checklist for your first session.</p>
<h2>Before you start (5 minutes)</h2>
<ul><li>Keep your student list ready in a spreadsheet: name, class, section, admission number, parent name and e-mail</li><li>List your subjects and teachers</li><li>Have the period timings handy</li></ul>
<h2>Set up (20 minutes)</h2>
<ol><li><b>Register the school</b> and wait for approval.</li><li><b>Create classes and sections</b> (for example 6-A, 6-B).</li><li><b>Add subjects</b> and then your faculty. Each teacher receives a temporary password by e-mail.</li><li><b>Import students</b> using a CSV file. Parent accounts are created automatically.</li><li><b>Build the timetable.</b> The system warns you if a teacher is double-booked.</li></ol>
<h2>Go live (5 minutes)</h2>
<ul><li>Ask each teacher to log in and change their password</li><li>Post a notice telling parents to expect absence e-mails</li><li>Mark the first period together and check that an alert arrives</li></ul>
<div><p><b>Tip:</b> run the new system next to the paper register for one week. When teachers see it is faster, the paper quietly disappears.</p></div>'],
 ['homework-and-recorded-lectures-help-absent-students', 'Homework and recorded lectures: helping absent students catch up', 'Missing a class should not mean missing the chapter.', 'Teaching',
  '<p>Every class has students who are absent due to illness, travel or family events. Without support, one missed day becomes weeks of confusion.</p>
<h2>A simple catch-up routine</h2>
<ol><li>The teacher posts the day\'s homework with any worksheet attached.</li><li>The teacher shares a short recorded explanation, notes (PDF) or a link to a trusted video.</li><li>The student watches at home and tries the homework.</li><li>Doubts are discussed at the start of the next class.</li></ol>
<h2>Making good lecture material</h2>
<ul><li>Keep videos short, ideally 5 to 15 minutes</li><li>One topic per video, with a clear title such as "Fractions - Chapter 3"</li><li>Add the notes or slides next to the video</li><li>Use a YouTube (unlisted) link for long recordings</li></ul>
<h2>Benefits for everyone</h2>
<p>Absent students do not fall behind. Regular students can revise before exams. Parents can see what was taught and help at home. Teachers answer fewer repeated questions.</p>
<blockquote>The best lecture library is not the biggest one. It is the one students actually open.</blockquote>'],
 ['ten-practices-to-protect-childrens-data', 'Protecting children\'s data: 10 practices every school should follow', 'Student records are sensitive. These habits make the biggest difference.', 'Security',
  '<p>Schools hold the personal details of minors. Protecting them is both a responsibility and, increasingly, a legal expectation.</p>
<h2>The ten practices</h2>
<ol><li><b>Collect only what you need.</b> If a feature does not need a field, do not ask for it.</li><li><b>Give each person a role</b> with the minimum permissions.</li><li><b>Use strong, unique passwords</b> and never share logins.</li><li><b>Change temporary passwords</b> on first login.</li><li><b>Log out on shared computers.</b></li><li><b>Keep records accurate</b> and correct mistakes quickly.</li><li><b>Never post student lists or photos</b> in open chat groups.</li><li><b>Share files only with the right class</b>, not through public links.</li><li><b>Review the audit log</b> now and then for anything unusual.</li><li><b>Delete data</b> you no longer need, and export a backup before you do.</li></ol>
<h2>Who is responsible?</h2>
<p>The software must provide safe defaults. The school must use them well. Both sides share the job.</p>
<p>Read our <a href="/compliance">Compliance &amp; Security</a> page for what the portal does, and the <a href="/privacy">privacy notice</a> for how data is handled.</p>'],
 ['what-inspectors-look-for-in-attendance-records', 'What education officers commonly look for in attendance records', 'Clean, consistent, easy-to-verify records make inspections calm.', 'Compliance',
  '<p>Rules differ by state and board, so always follow your own authority\'s guidance. That said, inspection teams tend to look for the same few things.</p>
<h2>Commonly checked points</h2>
<ul><li><b>Completeness</b> &mdash; is attendance recorded for every working day and class?</li><li><b>Consistency</b> &mdash; do totals add up, without overwriting or gaps?</li><li><b>Traceability</b> &mdash; who marked it, and when?</li><li><b>Percentages</b> &mdash; how many students are below the minimum threshold, and what was done?</li><li><b>Communication</b> &mdash; were parents informed of repeated absence?</li></ul>
<h2>How digital records help</h2>
<p>Each entry carries the date, period, subject and teacher. Corrections are limited to a short window and recorded. Percentages are calculated by the system, and exports can be handed over in minutes.</p>
<div><p><b>Important:</b> a portal supports good record-keeping, but it does not replace your obligations. Confirm requirements with your education department.</p></div>
<h2>Prepare a monthly habit</h2>
<ol><li>Review the below-threshold list every month.</li><li>Record what action was taken for each case.</li><li>Export and archive the report.</li></ol>'],
